<Base title="Community | The Common Squamish" description="The story behind The Common's Squamish community, from Peter's first note to members to the people and programs that shaped the space.">
  <header class="page-hd"><div class="wrap"><span class="eyebrow on-dark">Community at The Common</span><h1>Work together.<br />Grow together.</h1><p>A place to get your work done, and to know the people working beside you.</p></div></header>
  <section class="section light"><div class="wrap founding-grid"><div><span class="kicker">The idea from the start</span><h2>It started with a few founding members.</h2><p>The Common opened in 2017 in an original 1970s Squamish building. Peter Buchholz wanted people working on their own projects to have a place to focus and a reason to meet one another.</p><p>Just a few weeks after opening, he wrote a note to members. He was proud of the space, but felt he had spent too much time setting it up and too little time connecting the people inside it. He said plainly: <q>Our goal was to build community.</q></p><p>He invited members to a social, asked for their feedback, and set out to make more opportunities for them to meet. The Common has kept evolving around the people who use it.</p><a class="inline-link" href="/stories/first-weeks/">Peter's first weeks</a></div><aside class="founding-note"><span>November 2017</span><p>A member social. The first drink on Peter. An open invitation to say what could be better.</p><small>From Peter's first update to members</small></aside></div></section>
  <section class="section warm"><div class="wrap people"><span class="kicker">The people</span><h2>Different work. One room.</h2><p>Over the years, coders, lawyers, geospatial engineers, PhD students, green tech innovators, founders, and remote workers have all found a desk here. The mix keeps things interesting. You might come in to finish your own work and leave with a useful introduction, a fresh perspective, or a familiar face to say hello to tomorrow.</p><div class="people-details"><div><strong>Space to focus</strong><span>Desks, offices, and rooms for the work in front of you.</span></div><div><strong>Space to connect</strong><span>Lunch at the communal table, a chat in the kitchen, or a break outside.</span></div><div><strong>Space to contribute</strong><span>Ideas, introductions, and support for people building things in Squamish.</span></div></div></div></section>
  <section class="section light"><div class="wrap split"><div><span class="kicker">Where the idea came from</span><h2>What social capital means here.</h2><p>Seats2meet in Utrecht helped inspire The Common. Its idea of social capital is simple: people have more to offer one another than a fee for a desk. They have experience, time, contacts, and encouragement.</p><p>At The Common, that idea became Common Capital, gatherings for members, and support for local groups. Peter's vision was to make connections between people and organizations, not just rent out a room.</p><a class="inline-link" href="https://www.seats2meet.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">About Seats2meet</a></div><img class="photo" src="/img/story/event-lunch.jpg" alt="A shared lunch scene inspired by gatherings at The Common" loading="lazy" /></div></section>
  <section class="section warm"><div class="wrap feedback-grid"><div><span class="kicker">Still changing</span><h2>The space has always been a work in progress.</h2></div><div><p>Peter asked founding members what could be better. That habit shows up throughout The Common's old updates: new desks and monitors, more places for calls, changes to the lounge and kitchen, and work on the Micro Office. Some of the details have changed again since then. The habit of listening is what matters.</p><p>If something isn't working for you when you visit, tell us. The Common gets better when the people using it speak up.</p></div></div></section>
  <section class="section light"><div class="wrap community-grid"><article><span class="kicker">A regular workday</span><h2>The small things count.</h2><p>Lunch around the communal table. An informal social after work. A break in the garden. A well behaved dog asleep under the desk. Those everyday things help people feel at home here.</p></article><article><span class="kicker">For Squamish</span><h2>A place for people to gather.</h2><p>The Common has hosted meetups, film nights, conversations, and community events. In 2018, Peter announced an all candidates air hockey tournament for the local election, with proceeds intended for Howe Sound Women’s Shelter.</p><a class="inline-link" href="/event-space/">See the event space</a></article></div></section>
  <section class="section warm"><div class="wrap"><div class="program-intro"><span class="kicker">Part of The Common's history</span><h2>Making space for more people.</h2><p>Over the years, The Common has tried a few ways to help more people take part. These programs are part of our history. Funding and sponsorship are not confirmed current offers, so please ask us what is possible today.</p></div><div class="program-grid"><article><h3>Common Capital</h3><p>People could offer time, skills, or connections in exchange for more accessible desk space.</p></article><article><h3>Community grants</h3><p>Support was offered for Squamish groups working on small business, entrepreneurship, and innovation.</p></article><article><h3>Venue sponsorship</h3><p>Community groups and nonprofits could seek help with the cost of bringing people together.</p></article></div><div class="program-link"><a class="inline-link" href="/stories/community-programs/">Read how the programs worked</a><a class="inline-link" href="/contact/">Tell us about your idea</a></div></div></section>
  <section class="section light"><div class="wrap split"><img class="photo" src="/img/story/courtyard-second-view.jpg" alt="The Common outdoor courtyard" loading="lazy" /><div><span class="kicker">For locals and newcomers</span><h2>Come see if it feels right.</h2><p>Come work here for an hour or a day. Meet a few people, see how the space feels, and decide whether you'd like to come back. No need to choose a monthly desk before you've spent time here.</p><a class="btn btn-primary" href="/day-pass/">Book a desk</a></div></div></section>
</Base>
